Goal: one pull request on suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test that adds a new workspace packages/jsonl-lint and meets every acceptance line of https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/102. Today packages/ does not exist; the root package.json already declares workspaces packages/* and its test script runs npm test --workspaces --if-present; .github/workflows/test.yml runs npm ci then npm test on Node 22.

Steps:
1. Create packages/jsonl-lint/package.json: name "jsonl-lint", "type": "module", "scripts": { "test": "node --test" }, a "bin": { "jsonl-lint": "bin/jsonl-lint.js" }, engines node >=22, and no "dependencies" (no runtime dependencies; avoid devDependencies too, node:test needs none).
2. Create packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js (plain ES module, no deps) exporting lint(text) that returns an array of { line, error } objects, one per row that is not valid JSON:
   - split the text on \n (also strip a trailing \r so CRLF files work);
   - line numbers are 1-based and count every physical line, including blank ones;
   - skip lines that are empty or whitespace only;
   - a trailing newline at the end of the file does not create an extra row, but a last line with no terminating newline is still a row and is checked (a torn/truncated last row must be reported);
   - error is the JSON.parse error message string;
   - return [] when every row is valid.
3. Create packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js with a #!/usr/bin/env node shebang (make it executable, chmod +x, and commit the mode). Behaviour: with no argument, print a usage message to stderr and exit 2. If the file cannot be read (missing, ENOENT, or other read error), print a message to stderr and exit 2. Otherwise read the file as utf8, call lint, print one line per finding to stdout in exactly the form <file>:<line>: <error> (the file argument as given), exit 1 if there was any finding and 0 if none. Nothing goes to stdout for a clean file.
4. Create packages/jsonl-lint/test/ with node:test tests (e.g. test/lint.test.js; make sure `node --test` run from packages/jsonl-lint discovers them — files under test/ ending .test.js are found by default). Cover, at minimum, both through lint() and through the CLI (spawn process.execPath with bin/jsonl-lint.js via node:child_process, using temp files from node:fs/os):
   - a clean file: lint returns [], CLI exits 0 with empty stdout;
   - a bad middle row: e.g. 3 rows with row 2 broken, reported as line 2, CLI prints <file>:2: ... and exits 1;
   - a torn last row with no trailing newline (e.g. '{"a":1}\n{"b":' ), reported at its line number;
   - blank lines: blank/whitespace lines are skipped and do not shift numbering (a bad row after blanks keeps its physical 1-based line number);
   - exit codes: 0 clean, 1 findings, 2 for a missing file and 2 for no argument, and that the exit-2 cases write to stderr.
5. Create packages/jsonl-lint/README.md: what the tool does, usage (node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js <file>), the output format, exit codes 0/1/2, and the lint(text) API.
6. Update the root README.md: replace the "No tools yet: packages/ is empty" bullet under "What is here" with a bullet listing packages/jsonl-lint and a one-line description.
7. Run `npm install` at the repo root so package-lock.json records the new workspace, and commit the updated package-lock.json — otherwise `npm ci` in the test workflow fails. Then run `npm ci && npm test` at the root and confirm it passes and that the jsonl-lint tests actually ran.
8. Manually smoke-test the CLI: a clean file (exit 0), a file with a bad middle row and a torn last row (two lines of output, exit 1), a missing path (stderr, exit 2), no argument (stderr, exit 2).
9. Commit everything on your branch, push, and open one pull request to the default branch whose body contains the line: Closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/102
10. Wait for the test workflow (the "test" check from .github/workflows/test.yml) to finish on the PR head; if it fails, read the log, fix, push again, until it is green. Do not open a second PR.